  either the Notification Triggers API (Chrome-only, experimental) or Periodic Background Sync (Chrome-only, requires an
  installed PWA, and the browser decides when it runs). There's no cross-browser way to do it. Anything I build here is
  honest only if it's clear about when it can actually reach you.

  That leaves three genuinely different reaches:

  - [1mIn-page only[0m — an overdue badge and a banner inside the page. Works whenever you have the tab open and look at it.
    No permission prompt, no service worker, works in every browser. Costs: it can't interrupt you; you have to visit
    the page.
  - [1mOS notification while a tab is open[0m — the browser Notification API fires a real system notification, including when
    the tab is backgrounded behind other windows. Needs a one-time permission grant. Costs: if you close the tab,
    nothing fires. Realistically this means keeping the app pinned.
  - [1mInstalled PWA with a service worker[0m — closest to "reminds me even when I'm not in the browser," but Chrome-only for
    scheduling, requires installing the app, and the browser still throttles it. Significantly more machinery for a
    reliability ceiling that's still below a native reminders app.

  [1mMy recommendation: in-page badges plus OS notifications while a tab is open[0m, built so the in-page layer works on its
  own and the OS layer is a progressive enhancement on top. That gets you the real thing in most day-to-day use,
  degrades cleanly when permission is denied, and doesn't buy service-worker complexity for a guarantee it can't
  actually deliver. If you need reminders that reach you with the browser shut, the right answer is your OS's reminder
  app, not this — and I'd rather say that now than build a feature that silently doesn't fire.
